I received two responses, both from HP, regarding the corrupt HW =
databases.  The solution worked.  Many thanks to:

Phillip Brown and Jason Orendorf.

Below is Phillip's response.  Jason's was very similar.

Since you've got such a simple system, you can easily re-init the HW =
DBs. This will renumber all of your devices, so save that copy of your =
old hwmgr -show scsi output. You'll move new device special file names =
back to the old names after the initialization.

In single-user, after you issue the "mountroot", run:

# dn_setup -init
# dsfmgr -K

Note: If LSM is in use, then you'll need to recreate the mandatory LSM =
devices
you've just deleted with the init. Do that like this:

  # mknod /dev/volconfig c 41 0
  # mknod /dev/voltrace c 41 1
  # mknod /dev/voliod c 41 2
  # mknod /dev/volinfo c 41 3

The following should return no errors if this is successful.=20

# dsfmgr -v  =20

You should be able to bcheckrc and come up normally. I'd reboot normally =
as a further check. If it fails, let me know. We'll supply you with a =
procedure for re-making your hardware databases from scratch.

Phil

